---
Part II: The shape of a loom
Chapter 1: The first syntax
Language: TypeScript
---

# The first syntax

At this stage we have no Loom-specific syntax at all. This chapter is the genesis of its own: it must name Loom's foundational primitives before it can be read. Loom is written in TypeScript with Effect, in pure functional style — but the syntax it defines is its own.

The very first thing that lets us materialise a Loom program is the *arrow*. Arrows have a simple and crucial role: they map *prose* to *code*.

=>

// Hello! This is the beginning of the Loom language.

~

Our very first program does nothing but show the rhythm of every Loom program. Its one line is a comment, and the next chapter, "The node foundation", begins Loom's first file with it. As you noticed, a *tilde* `~` marks the way back from code to prose, as the arrow marked the way in. A section holds any number of code and prose blocks, divided by the arrows and the tildes. The next heading ends a block of code as well, so a tilde is needed only to return to prose within the same section.

That is the whole rhythm. The rest of Loom's syntax is a handful of marks, each small enough to show whole.

A loom is written in chapters, and each chapter is a file of its own. It opens with frontmatter: a few lines between two `---` fences that say where the chapter sits and what its code is written in. The chapter that opens a part names the part on one line and itself on the next.

```loom
---
Part II: The shape of a loom
Chapter 1: The first syntax
Language: TypeScript
---
```

A later chapter names its part by numeral alone, as in `Part II, Chapter 2: The node foundation`. It may also add a `Package` line, naming the file or the directory its code is written to.

A heading of one to six `#` opens a section, and the section runs on to the next heading. Under it, prose and code take turns. An arrow opens each block of code, and a tilde closes it when more prose follows in the same section.

```loom
# A greeting

The greeting needs a name.

=>

const name = 'world'

~

Then it says hello.
```

Some code writes lines that would otherwise open a section: the titles of a Markdown file, or a comment in a shell script. Such code is fenced, the way Markdown fences code. Three backticks on the line after the arrow open the fence, and three more close it. Every line between them is code, and the prose after the closing fence needs no tilde.

````loom
=>
```md
# Loom

Programs written to be read.
```

Prose again.
````

A heading can say where its code goes and what Loom should make of it. A file name in brackets is a sink, the file that section's code is written to. A word in braces is a specifier. `{Tangle}` writes a section to the file its chapter's `Package` line names, so a chapter that makes one file needs no sink. A language name such as `{Bash}` switches the section's language.

```loom
## The module [greeting.ts]

## Installing it {Bash}
```

Inside code, an anchor draws in another section by its title, written `::[Name]`. Tangling puts the named section's code where the anchor stood.

```loom
## The greeting [greeting.ts]

=>

::[A greeting]

console.log(`hello ${name}`)
```

A warp binds a value to a name, written in the prose before a section's first arrow. An anchor with the same name then draws that value into the code, and the author writes any quotes around the anchor.

```loom
## The salutation

{{ salutation = "hello" }}

=>

const salutation = '::[salutation]'
```

Two more marks set text apart from Loom altogether. A fence in prose opens a quote, and every line up to the fence that closes it is shown verbatim, so its arrows and headings stay text. That is how this chapter shows its examples without composing them. Only a fence at the same indent and at least as long closes another, so the quote of fenced code above opens with four backticks and holds the three inside it. A box runs from a line of three colons to a line of three colons alone. The opening line may name the box and give it a kind in braces, as `::: Why a grid {Note}` does, and a box that names no kind is a note, a remark for whoever opens the loom next. Tangling drops a box, so it never reaches the code.

Loom does not work on this text directly. It reads the text into a tree, and every later pass reads that tree and adds to it. The chapters that follow give the tree its shape: first the node every part of it is built from, then the tokens inside a line, the lines themselves, the sections they form, and the corpus above them.
